<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lezgins</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Lezgin people</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lezgins are a Caucasian people of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagestan">Dagestan</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, living mainly in the Samur valley and the neighbouring mountains. They speak Lezgian, a Northeast Caucasian language, and are mostly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> Muslims. In Georgian and Persian sources the name Lezgin was often applied loosely to all the mountaineers of Dagestan who raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> in the 17th and 18th centuries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lezgins.jpg" alt="Lezgins" width="320" height="224" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zurgh · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lezgian_flag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
